1
00:01:52,841 --> 00:01:55,841
THE LAND ON THE FAR BANK

2
00:02:14,281 --> 00:02:16,441
Are the fish biting?

3
00:02:17,741 --> 00:02:20,321
The fish are already in hibernation, right?

4
00:02:22,321 --> 00:02:24,541
I don't care if I don't catch anything.

5
00:02:24,871 --> 00:02:27,081
My real job is operating the ferry.

6
00:02:28,291 --> 00:02:30,421
You guys are Danes, right?

7
00:02:30,751 --> 00:02:33,751
You speak good English,

8
00:02:34,301 --> 00:02:36,461
but you have a bit of an accent.

9
00:02:37,011 --> 00:02:39,511
Your accent sounds just like people from my country.

10
00:02:40,971 --> 00:02:43,931
How much would it cost to take us to
the opposite shore?

11
00:02:44,641 --> 00:02:45,851
Two silver pieces.

12
00:02:46,521 --> 00:02:49,811
If you don't have silver, I also take alcohol.
Well, then...

13
00:02:50,351 --> 00:02:53,771
I wonder how many days it would take me to bring
all of you.

14
00:02:55,441 --> 00:02:57,991
No. I only need you to bring this letter.

15
00:03:00,031 --> 00:03:03,071
I thought you were customers. That's too bad.

16
00:03:04,161 --> 00:03:07,241
So, who do you want me to deliver it to?

17
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:10,121
To any one of the elders. It's urgent.

18
00:03:10,751 --> 00:03:13,961
Please tell them it's from Askeladd,
son of Lydia.

19
00:03:22,891 --> 00:03:25,221
Understood. I'll hurry.

20
00:03:47,411 --> 00:03:48,661
Askeladd!

21
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:52,791
Askeladd! Where are you? Hey!

22
00:03:53,171 --> 00:03:55,881
Ragnar. Please be quiet.

23
00:03:56,131 --> 00:03:57,541
Why did you stop the march?

24
00:03:58,001 --> 00:04:00,131
Thorkell is going to catch up!

25
00:04:00,801 --> 00:04:04,641
We're not supposed to make noise
while The Ear is at work.

26
00:04:07,301 --> 00:04:08,351
Ear?

27
00:04:09,101 --> 00:04:10,021
Bjorn.

28
00:04:10,181 --> 00:04:12,391
Don't let that conehead breathe.

29
00:04:13,391 --> 00:04:15,731
Ear! What’s the status?

30
00:04:17,021 --> 00:04:19,071
They've gotten closer since yesterday.

31
00:04:19,731 --> 00:04:22,861
I can hear soldiers and horses marching.

32
00:04:23,531 --> 00:04:24,951
There's about 500.

33
00:04:25,861 --> 00:04:27,831
They're close behind us.

34
00:04:28,201 --> 00:04:29,661
They're only one day away on foot.

35
00:04:30,331 --> 00:04:31,621
Are you serious?

36
00:04:32,291 --> 00:04:34,751
Yesterday, you said they were two days away.

37
00:04:35,421 --> 00:04:37,291
You don't trust my ears?

38
00:04:37,501 --> 00:04:38,631
That was fast.

39
00:04:39,291 --> 00:04:41,341
They're marching fast.

40
00:04:42,211 --> 00:04:45,091
They'll catch up to us in just a few days.

41
00:04:47,301 --> 00:04:48,641
Quiet, you guys.

42
00:04:54,141 --> 00:04:56,901
Horses. On the hill. Two of them.

43
00:05:05,571 --> 00:05:07,411
They've got us now.

44
00:05:07,951 --> 00:05:08,911
What do we do?

45
00:05:09,281 --> 00:05:11,991
If we're going to meet them
in battle, we must take positions!

46
00:05:12,241 --> 00:05:15,331
No, that's impossible.
We're no match for them.

47
00:05:15,541 --> 00:05:17,041
But if we don't do anything...

48
00:05:17,831 --> 00:05:19,541
I've sent a request for reinforcements.

49
00:05:19,881 --> 00:05:22,131
We'll keep running until they arrive.

50
00:05:22,801 --> 00:05:24,261
-What?
-Reinforcements?

51
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:38,691
NOVEMBER 1013 AD WEST ENGLAND
DOWNSTREAM EAST BANK OF SEVERN RIVER

52
00:05:38,811 --> 00:05:39,771
Reinforcements?

53
00:05:40,441 --> 00:05:42,021
We're deep in enemy territory!

54
00:05:42,481 --> 00:05:44,231
That's what Askeladd said, though.

55
00:05:44,651 --> 00:05:47,071
He's never lied to us about something like that before.

56
00:05:48,241 --> 00:05:49,701
Well, you're right.

57
00:05:50,321 --> 00:05:52,121
But where's the closest fort?

58
00:05:52,201 --> 00:05:53,241
It's Leicester.

59
00:05:53,451 --> 00:05:56,041
Seriously? It'll take two weeks to get there!

60
00:05:56,581 --> 00:05:58,711
I wonder if the reinforcements will be in time.

61
00:05:58,921 --> 00:06:02,041
If we ask for reinforcements, we have to
split the plunder, right?

62
00:06:02,131 --> 00:06:05,801
Askeladd said we could handle it ourselves
with just the hundred of us.

63
00:06:05,961 --> 00:06:09,341
I'm so tired! I want to get back on a ship!

64
00:06:09,431 --> 00:06:11,051
Yeah.

65
00:06:11,351 --> 00:06:13,141
A ship would be nice...

66
00:06:40,291 --> 00:06:42,711
What? Do you need something?

67
00:06:48,511 --> 00:06:50,261
Everyone's worried.

68
00:06:50,881 --> 00:06:52,641
You should give them an explanation.

69
00:06:55,891 --> 00:06:57,431
Let them worry.

70
00:06:57,981 --> 00:07:01,481
Worrying will make them march faster.

71
00:07:04,271 --> 00:07:05,271
What?

72
00:07:05,821 --> 00:07:07,691
If that's all, then go to sleep.

73
00:07:09,111 --> 00:07:13,951
You've been acting weird the last few days.
What are you up to?

74
00:07:17,951 --> 00:07:20,711
Ever since you saw the prince's face. Right?

75
00:07:26,211 --> 00:07:30,551
I've been living among criminals
for 40 years.

76
00:07:31,681 --> 00:07:33,551
I can tell what a man is like,

77
00:07:33,641 --> 00:07:36,311
whether he's a big shot or a nobody,
whether he's smart

78
00:07:36,391 --> 00:07:39,221
or stupid, just by looking at his face.

79
00:07:44,691 --> 00:07:48,691
When I saw the prince's face, I could tell.

80
00:07:50,441 --> 00:07:52,911
"That's not the face of a king."

81
00:07:54,871 --> 00:07:57,781
True. He looks like a girl.

82
00:07:58,161 --> 00:08:01,001
But he's still a kid, you know?

83
00:08:02,871 --> 00:08:07,541
That's true. He's still young. His future still awaits.

84
00:08:07,841 --> 00:08:09,631
What? His future still awaits?

85
00:08:09,711 --> 00:08:11,761
All right! Wake up, guys!

86
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:16,221
Get ready to march! If you're slow,
Thorkell is going to catch up!

87
00:08:18,391 --> 00:08:19,811
Seriously?

88
00:08:20,101 --> 00:08:22,891
It's still dark.

89
00:09:07,521 --> 00:09:08,861
Hey.

90
00:09:09,691 --> 00:09:12,151
-Yeah?
-Were you able to sleep?

91
00:09:12,731 --> 00:09:13,651
No.

92
00:09:15,741 --> 00:09:16,701
Hey.

93
00:09:16,781 --> 00:09:19,201
Shut up. Be quiet.

94
00:09:19,621 --> 00:09:21,701
But if I don't keep talking,

95
00:09:21,791 --> 00:09:23,831
I'm going to fall asleep.

96
00:09:27,541 --> 00:09:30,921
This is bad, Askeladd. They're right behind us.

97
00:09:31,001 --> 00:09:32,841
Yeah. I know.

98
00:09:32,921 --> 00:09:34,671
They're going to catch up.

99
00:09:34,761 --> 00:09:36,971
I said, I know.

100
00:09:37,051 --> 00:09:40,971
We're almost to where the reinforcements will come.
Right there.

101
00:09:55,691 --> 00:09:58,911
-Reinforcements?
-Them? Just the two of them?

102
00:09:58,991 --> 00:10:01,121
Maybe it's just an illusion made by the fog.

103
00:10:18,051 --> 00:10:21,761
I'm so honored that the commander has come himself.

104
00:10:23,681 --> 00:10:26,311
It's been a long time, Askeladd.

105
00:11:03,051 --> 00:11:04,011
So,

106
00:11:04,391 --> 00:11:08,311
you're saying a ship carried them across the river?

107
00:11:08,891 --> 00:11:10,641
Do you know whose ship it was?

108
00:11:10,891 --> 00:11:12,731
No. I'm not sure.

109
00:11:13,021 --> 00:11:16,071
The fog was as thick as goat's milk.

110
00:11:16,571 --> 00:11:20,611
It wasn't a Norse ship. It didn't have a dragonhead.

111
00:11:20,861 --> 00:11:23,571
It was kind of pudgy.

112
00:11:23,911 --> 00:11:27,701
It had an eye drawn on it. On the bow. Big, like this.

113
00:11:29,121 --> 00:11:31,581
That doesn't sound like one of King Sweyn's ships.

114
00:11:32,331 --> 00:11:36,801
In any case, we can't follow Thorfinn anymore.

115
00:11:38,461 --> 00:11:41,091
I underestimated them.

116
00:11:41,761 --> 00:11:45,761
I didn't think that the people across the Severn River
were their allies.

117
00:11:46,891 --> 00:11:51,441
Does that mean the people across the river
switched sides from England to Denmark?

118
00:11:51,811 --> 00:11:54,481
You idiot! You don't know anything!

119
00:11:55,021 --> 00:11:59,481
The land on the other side of this river isn't
part of England in the first place!

120
00:12:01,071 --> 00:12:02,451
It's Wales.

121
00:12:05,991 --> 00:12:10,831
Wales is a mountainous region to the west of England.

122
00:12:11,831 --> 00:12:14,581
The steep mountains had few natural resources.

123
00:12:14,831 --> 00:12:17,131
Flat land suitable for agriculture was scarce.

124
00:12:18,041 --> 00:12:21,341
The land was poor compared to England.

125
00:12:22,381 --> 00:12:24,931
After the end of rule by the Roman Empire,

126
00:12:25,011 --> 00:12:28,471
Wales was never unified under a single country.

127
00:12:29,221 --> 00:12:34,101
The region was divided into many
small, rival kingdoms for hundreds of years.

128
00:12:35,401 --> 00:12:37,941
Askeladd's request for reinforcements was answered

129
00:12:38,021 --> 00:12:40,651
by one of those small kingdoms.

130
00:12:42,571 --> 00:12:46,161
EAST OF MORGANNWG KINGDOM
WALES

131
00:12:46,571 --> 00:12:48,831
I want to compliment you for this, Askeladd.

132
00:12:48,911 --> 00:12:53,001
Thorkell must be clenching his teeth in anger
on the other side of the river.

133
00:12:53,581 --> 00:12:55,671
If we continue going by sea,

134
00:12:55,871 --> 00:12:58,001
we can return to Gainsborough.

135
00:12:58,461 --> 00:13:00,841
No. We're going to go by land.

136
00:13:01,591 --> 00:13:04,971
What are you talking about?
There's a ship right there.

137
00:13:05,591 --> 00:13:08,511
Don't look at it from the perspective
of a large nation.

138
00:13:09,431 --> 00:13:13,981
For this country, even three warships
are a valuable asset.

139
00:13:14,641 --> 00:13:18,731
We have to be thankful that they helped us
escape from Thorkell in the first place.

140
00:13:19,151 --> 00:13:21,901
Then we can make do with only two ships!
We can all fit!

141
00:13:22,071 --> 00:13:25,281
No. If you keep acting selfish,
I'm going to leave you behind.

142
00:13:25,951 --> 00:13:29,491
Are you telling me to walk across Wales?
That's ridiculous!

143
00:13:29,991 --> 00:13:32,371
The people in this land hate us!

144
00:13:33,041 --> 00:13:36,671
I can't allow you to endanger His Highness any further!

145
00:13:40,091 --> 00:13:41,171
A hawk.

146
00:13:42,131 --> 00:13:43,261
It's so low.

147
00:13:44,461 --> 00:13:46,221
Ragnar. Catch it.

148
00:13:46,881 --> 00:13:49,931
Pardon me, Your Highness, we're in the middle
of something important.

149
00:13:51,301 --> 00:13:55,351
It's better than moving north through England
with Thorkell at our heels.

150
00:13:56,061 --> 00:13:58,731
Anyway, this country is an ally of mine.

151
00:13:59,691 --> 00:14:01,571
If you're still worried...

152
00:14:02,401 --> 00:14:03,231
Thorfinn!

153
00:14:07,241 --> 00:14:10,031
I'll assign him to guard Canute.

154
00:14:10,871 --> 00:14:13,331
Wow! You're the guy from back there...

155
00:14:14,871 --> 00:14:16,541
Don't make the decision for me.

156
00:14:17,461 --> 00:14:19,791
He's small, but he's good.

157
00:14:19,961 --> 00:14:23,171
And he's the same age as His Highness.
I'm sure they'll get along.

158
00:14:23,461 --> 00:14:25,171
Say your greetings, Thorfinn.

159
00:14:33,891 --> 00:14:37,391
Are you an idiot?
What are you glaring at him for?

160
00:14:39,021 --> 00:14:41,021
You're going to reward me for this, right?

161
00:14:41,231 --> 00:14:45,031
All right, all right.
When we get to Gainsborough, I'll duel you.

162
00:14:45,981 --> 00:14:48,191
Askeladd. Are you ready?

163
00:14:48,281 --> 00:14:51,821
Yes. We just finished.
Oh, my. We're so busy.

164
00:14:54,451 --> 00:14:58,791
This is the general of the Kingdom of Morgannwg,
Gratianus.

165
00:14:58,871 --> 00:15:01,461
He's going to show us the way.

166
00:15:05,211 --> 00:15:08,471
You have a threatening look about you,
but you have a good face.

167
00:15:09,221 --> 00:15:12,301
Commander, that's one of my men.

168
00:15:12,721 --> 00:15:15,311
His Highness Prince Canute is over there.

169
00:15:19,521 --> 00:15:20,891
His eyes look weak.

170
00:15:21,191 --> 00:15:24,021
Is he really going to be the next ruler of England?

171
00:15:24,111 --> 00:15:26,571
What? You bastard! How rude!

172
00:15:27,611 --> 00:15:32,571
There's only one reason
we're helping the Danish prince escape.

173
00:15:32,661 --> 00:15:35,871
It's so that we can enter a treaty of non-aggression.

174
00:15:36,871 --> 00:15:40,831
It's all up to you. Prince, swear

175
00:15:41,291 --> 00:15:43,711
that once you become the king of England,

176
00:15:43,791 --> 00:15:46,801
you'll never interfere in the matters of Wales.

177
00:15:59,061 --> 00:16:00,481
Yes. Leave it to me.

178
00:16:01,641 --> 00:16:04,271
His Highness says,

179
00:16:04,351 --> 00:16:06,861
"If you give us three ships--"

180
00:16:06,981 --> 00:16:09,861
I asked the prince.

181
00:16:09,981 --> 00:16:11,111
What?

182
00:16:16,871 --> 00:16:19,621
Never mind. I'll ask again later.

183
00:16:22,711 --> 00:16:25,671
I'll prepare a written document in the meantime.

184
00:16:25,751 --> 00:16:28,131
Get ready to depart.

185
00:16:31,761 --> 00:16:34,091
What's wrong with that arrogant snob?

186
00:16:34,181 --> 00:16:35,931
He's taking advantage of us!

187
00:16:36,511 --> 00:16:39,891
Does he think he's an equal to the prince?

188
00:16:40,181 --> 00:16:41,681
That country bumpkin!

189
00:16:50,281 --> 00:16:54,861
Marching, marching, and more marching!
Now we're marching in the mountains!

190
00:16:54,951 --> 00:16:57,781
Mountains are better than Thorkell.

191
00:16:57,871 --> 00:16:59,911
Let's talk about something more fun.

192
00:16:59,991 --> 00:17:01,331
Like what?

193
00:17:01,701 --> 00:17:04,831
Our reward from King Sweyn.

194
00:17:04,911 --> 00:17:08,331
We saved the prince from Thorkell.

195
00:17:08,461 --> 00:17:11,171
I want a farm!

196
00:17:11,251 --> 00:17:12,711
I want a cool sword.

197
00:17:13,131 --> 00:17:17,681
No. It's all about the gold. Nothing's better than gold.

198
00:17:17,761 --> 00:17:18,801
Yeah.

199
00:17:18,891 --> 00:17:20,761
I heard the king has

200
00:17:20,851 --> 00:17:24,851
a hundred beautiful slave women
from around the world in his bedroom.

201
00:17:27,601 --> 00:17:29,151
Come here.

202
00:17:29,271 --> 00:17:32,981
I'm so excited! Is there anything else?

203
00:17:33,361 --> 00:17:34,901
Hey, priest!

204
00:17:35,401 --> 00:17:37,741
You've been to the palace, right?

205
00:17:37,821 --> 00:17:40,121
What does the king have in his house?

206
00:17:43,081 --> 00:17:47,871
Well, there was nothing of value in particular.

207
00:17:48,001 --> 00:17:49,961
No way! Not even beautiful women?

208
00:17:50,041 --> 00:17:53,341
Well, he did have a lot of slave women.

209
00:17:54,091 --> 00:17:56,671
What? Aren't you interested?

210
00:17:56,761 --> 00:17:59,141
You don't like women?

211
00:17:59,551 --> 00:18:03,181
No. Gold, silver, and beautiful women are all nothing

212
00:18:03,261 --> 00:18:06,851
compared to the thing I seek.

213
00:18:10,481 --> 00:18:13,651
Please tell us! What is it you're seeking?

214
00:18:17,701 --> 00:18:19,031
Love.

215
00:18:19,161 --> 00:18:21,781
-What? Love?
-Yes.

216
00:18:21,911 --> 00:18:24,661
What's that? I've never heard of it.

217
00:18:24,741 --> 00:18:28,791
I know what it is. It's like a spell
that the Christians are always chanting.

218
00:18:28,921 --> 00:18:30,331
Isn't it some kind of food?

219
00:18:30,421 --> 00:18:32,541
How many pounds in silver?

220
00:18:33,251 --> 00:18:35,381
It can't be measured in silver,

221
00:18:35,461 --> 00:18:38,301
because love is what gives value to silver.

222
00:18:39,341 --> 00:18:44,061
Without love, gold, silver, and beautiful women
are all worthless.

223
00:18:44,511 --> 00:18:45,891
"Gives value"?

224
00:18:45,971 --> 00:18:48,181
That doesn't make sense.

225
00:18:48,271 --> 00:18:50,521
Silver has the value of silver.

226
00:18:50,601 --> 00:18:52,861
Don't make it so difficult.

227
00:18:52,981 --> 00:18:54,521
He's bluffing.

228
00:18:54,611 --> 00:18:56,111
Just ignore him.

229
00:19:00,071 --> 00:19:01,911
Hey, priest.

230
00:19:03,321 --> 00:19:07,251
Keep talking. I want to hear more.

231
00:19:10,211 --> 00:19:13,081
We're about to enter the Kingdom of Brycheiniog.

232
00:19:14,501 --> 00:19:17,211
Don't worry. I've talked to them already.

233
00:19:17,631 --> 00:19:20,011
Thank you for your help.

234
00:19:20,091 --> 00:19:23,641
We're not doing this for you.
It's just that our interests are aligned.

235
00:19:24,351 --> 00:19:26,721
The Welsh kingdoms have always been

236
00:19:26,811 --> 00:19:29,981
threatened by invasions from England.

237
00:19:30,061 --> 00:19:33,401
If you Danes can make them stop,

238
00:19:33,481 --> 00:19:35,021
it would be good for us.

239
00:19:35,901 --> 00:19:39,491
I'm sure His Highness the Prince will promise you that.

240
00:19:42,861 --> 00:19:44,871
So, is that your king, Askeladd?

241
00:19:46,281 --> 00:19:48,371
He's from a good bloodline.

242
00:19:48,451 --> 00:19:51,711
As for the rest, I have high hopes for him
since he's young.

243
00:19:55,631 --> 00:19:59,551
The more you fall for a man,
the less things go how you want.

244
00:20:00,591 --> 00:20:05,181
Maybe someone like him is just what we need.

245
00:20:07,181 --> 00:20:10,641
We're the descendants of Britannia.

246
00:20:11,101 --> 00:20:13,231
We're not going to follow the king of the Danes.

247
00:20:14,481 --> 00:20:16,061
Gratianus.

248
00:20:16,611 --> 00:20:19,531
Do you still believe in the legend?

249
00:20:20,321 --> 00:20:24,031
The legend that Artorius will return
from Avalon to the west

250
00:20:24,111 --> 00:20:28,331
and restore the Britannia of old.

251
00:20:35,381 --> 00:20:36,211
Askeladd!

252
00:20:39,301 --> 00:20:40,591
Are you sure?

253
00:20:40,671 --> 00:20:42,381
That's what The Ear says.

254
00:20:44,551 --> 00:20:47,681
Thank you for coming, prince of the Danes!

255
00:20:53,431 --> 00:20:55,731
They're messengers from Brycheiniog.

256
00:20:56,311 --> 00:20:57,441
Thank you for coming!

257
00:20:57,901 --> 00:21:00,191
Wait! Gratianus.

258
00:21:03,701 --> 00:21:05,201
Did they notice?

259
00:21:05,321 --> 00:21:06,861
How can you tell?

260
00:21:06,951 --> 00:21:09,331
One of my men has good hearing.

261
00:21:10,031 --> 00:21:12,201
But it's too late.

262
00:21:16,001 --> 00:21:17,211
What's wrong, Thorfinn?

263
00:22:18,641 --> 00:22:21,021
Get up! Hurry up!

264
00:22:23,861 --> 00:22:24,691
Let's go.
